            },
            {
                "module_name": "factory_reset",
                "module_filename": "factory_reset.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "factory_reset",
                        "tablename": "factory_reset",
                        "name": "Factory Reset",
                        "description": "Reports the factory_reset bootstat record; AOSP stores the event value in the file's modification time. bootstat writes this record with the device's current time when it runs and finds no factory_reset record (RecordFactoryReset in bootstat.cpp at tag android-14.0.0_r1, lines 1428 to 1431), so the value is the time of that run by the device clock. The time shown is the modification time the extraction recorded for the file.",
                        "author": "Kevin Pagano (@stark4n6), @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2022-01-05",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Timestamp is the modification time the extraction recorded for the file as an epoch value, shown in UTC: the NTFS field (0x000a), or else the extended timestamp field (0x5455), of a zip member, the mtime of a tar member, or the file's own modification time when the input is a folder. It is blank when the extraction recorded no such value. Archive Time Modified (No Zone) is filled only in that case, for a zip member, with the date and time in the member's zip directory entry, shown as stored. A zip directory entry records no time zone, so that reading is not converted and which zone it is in is not established. One row is reported per matched file. On the registered images checked on 2026-10-04, each held one matched file; the zip member carried the extended timestamp on anne_a15, galaxys10_a10, hc_pixel8pro_a16, kevin_pocox7_a15, pixel7a_a14, samsunga53_a14 and russell_pixel6a_a13, where the zip directory reading was within 1 second of a whole number of hours from it (2 hours later on galaxys10_a10, 4 or 5 hours earlier on the other six); it carried none on samsungs20_a13 and sharon_a14, whose zips record the NTFS field instead, and on runs of 2026-10-23 the zip directory reading there was 4 hours earlier than it to within 1 second; userb2_a13 is a tar. Extraction and acquisition handling can disturb file timestamps, so validate the value against other sources. Reference: AOSP bootstat, 'boot_event_record_store.cpp (event values are stored in the file mtime attribute)', https://android.googlesource.com/platform/system/core/+/refs/tags/android-14.0.0_r1/bootstat/boot_event_record_store.cpp (lines 66 to 91)",
                        "category": "Wipe & Setup"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "Threema",
                "module_filename": "Threema.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "threema_account",
                        "tablename": "threema_account",
                        "name": "Threema - Account",
                        "description": "The signed-in Threema account's own Threema ID, display nickname, and any mobile number linked to it, read from the app's main preferences file.",
                        "author": "@Gear-I & Claude, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "notes": "The account is reported by its Threema ID as stored in the preferences file. 'Linked Mobile Number' is reported when the preferences file carries one and is blank otherwise; absence is not evidence that no number was ever linked.",
                        "category": "Threema"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "threema_contacts",
                        "tablename": "threema_contacts",
                        "name": "Threema - Contacts",
                        "description": "Contacts recorded in Threema's local, SQLCipher-encrypted database, with each one's stored verification level and the date the contact record was created locally.",
                        "author": "@Gear-I & Claude, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Threema encrypts its main database (threema4.db) at rest with SQLCipher; this module decrypts it in memory before reading it, using the version 1 key.dat format in Threema's published source (a fixed 32-byte XOR mask followed by a SHA-1 checksum verification). Reference: Threema for Android, 'Version1MasterKeyFileManager.kt', https://github.com/threema-ch/threema-android/blob/d44a2b53b2ea2c0bf1065bdaa96987570e5b4e6d/app/src/main/java/ch/threema/localcrypto/Version1MasterKeyFileManager.kt#L18-L43; the format is also described at github.com/wilzbach/threema-decrypt. On pixel7a_a14 the checksum embedded in key.dat matched the derived key, and the decrypted database opened and returned the contact and message rows recorded in sample_data. This covers only a version 1 key.dat with no passphrase protection. The client's current source also defines version 2 master key files, master_key.dat and enc_master_key_v2.dat, which this module does not read; an install that has no key.dat produces no rows, and its threema4.db is listed as a source file and left undecrypted. Reference: Threema for Android, 'MasterKeyFileProvider.kt', https://github.com/threema-ch/threema-android/blob/d44a2b53b2ea2c0bf1065bdaa96987570e5b4e6d/app/src/main/java/ch/threema/localcrypto/MasterKeyFileProvider.kt#L8-L20. The no-key.dat case was exercised on a copy of the pixel7a_a14 files with key.dat removed, not on an install that carries a version 2 file. A passphrase-protected key.dat is also left undecrypted (key.dat's own first byte, read directly rather than assumed, records this; on a device where it indicates passphrase protection this module leaves the database undecrypted rather than guess at a passphrase-derived key). The database is opened strictly read-only, and its write-ahead log (threema4.db-wal) is opened alongside it so that any transaction committed to the log but not yet checkpointed into the main file is still included - on the device this was validated against, the main file alone was several weeks stale and missing real, later content that only existed in the log. 'Verification Level' maps the stored verificationLevel value as 0=Unverified, 1=Server-verified, 2=Fully verified; the names are those of the client's VerificationLevel list (Reference: Threema for Android, 'VerificationLevel.java', https://github.com/threema-ch/threema-android/blob/d44a2b53b2ea2c0bf1065bdaa96987570e5b4e6d/domain/src/main/java/ch/threema/domain/models/VerificationLevel.java#L10-L12). How a contact reached a level is not stored in this column and is not reported. On the device this was validated against, the one contact recorded at level 2 corresponds exactly to a 'via QR scan' verification event documented for that same contact. 'Group-Only / Removed' reflects Threema's own AcquaintanceLevel field on the contact record; the column shows Yes when the stored acquaintanceLevel is set and not 0. Threema's Android source defines an acquaintance level named GROUP_OR_DELETED as covering three cases: a contact who is in a group with the user, was not explicitly added and has no 1:1 conversation; a contact who was in a group with the user before, where either of them has since been removed from all common groups; and a contact the user explicitly removed (Reference: Threema for Android, 'Contact.kt (AcquaintanceLevel)', https://github.com/threema-ch/threema-android/blob/d44a2b53b2ea2c0bf1065bdaa96987570e5b4e6d/domain/src/main/java/ch/threema/domain/models/Contact.kt#L213-L231) - the three cases cannot be told apart from this field alone, so they are reported together rather than guessing which applies. 'Date Created' is the stored dateCreated value, read as Unix milliseconds; the client's source describes that column as the date when the contact was created locally (Reference: Threema for Android, 'ContactModel.java', https://github.com/threema-ch/threema-android/blob/d44a2b53b2ea2c0bf1065bdaa96987570e5b4e6d/app/src/main/java/ch/threema/storage/models/ContactModel.java#L57).",
                        "category": "Threema"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "threema_messages",
                        "tablename": "threema_messages",
                        "name": "Threema - Messages",
                        "description": "One-to-one messages recorded in Threema's local, SQLCipher-encrypted database: text, file (type 8), static location, and call events, with direction, delivery/read status, and any quote-reply relationship between messages.",
                        "author": "@Gear-I & Claude, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-06",
                        "notes": "Decrypted the same way as Threema - Contacts, including reading the database's write-ahead log alongside the main file; see that artifact's notes for the key.dat/SQLCipher method and its passphrase-protected limitation. On the device this was validated against, a pair of static-location messages exchanged in one conversation existed only in the write-ahead log and would have been missed entirely by reading the main database file alone. Only the four message-type combinations confirmed on this device are decoded: plain text; a file message, type 8 (FILE) in the client's MessageType list (threema-android MessageType.kt at commit d44a2b53, https://github.com/threema-ch/threema-android/blob/d44a2b53b2ea2c0bf1065bdaa96987570e5b4e6d/app/src/main/java/ch/threema/storage/models/MessageType.kt#L23-L28), reported as File; read the MIME Type column to see what kind of file a row holds (filename, MIME type and file size are read from the message's own structured content, matched by value rather than by trusting a fixed field position, since that content is a positional JSON array with no field names of its own); a static location (latitude, longitude, accuracy in metres and a resolved address, read by fixed field position, since this content's structure - unlike the file case - showed the same fixed [latitude, longitude, accuracy, address] shape on every location message observed); and a call event (status/call ID/duration are read by name, since that part of the structure is a JSON object). Every call on this device carried the same numeric call-type marker regardless of whether it was really an audio or a video call, so this module does not report an audio/video distinction rather than guess at one; duration and direction are still reported. 'Quoted Message' is resolved via the replying message's own quotedMessageId against the original message's apiMessageId (the message's protocol id, which is distinct from the database's own row id), not by row order. Group messages, distribution lists and polls are out of scope for this release: every corresponding table was empty on the device this was validated against, so their column mapping could not be confirmed against real content. A message can be missing from this table with a gap in the numbering between its neighbours; this module does not assert deletion from a gap alone since a gap has other possible causes (e.g. a message never finishing sync before extraction), and the database's own deletedAtUtc/editedAtUtc columns, which would confirm a deletion or edit directly, were unset on every row on this device.",
                        "category": "Threema"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "whatsAppExtended",
                "module_filename": "whatsAppExtended.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_whatsapp_reactions",
                        "tablename": "get_whatsapp_reactions",
                        "name": "WhatsApp - Message Reactions",
                        "description": "WhatsApp emoji reactions to messages (msgstore.db message_add_on_reaction)",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Codex, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-14",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Each row is one entry in the message_add_on_reaction table joined to its message_add_on record and the message it reacted to. In every tested image the add-on record's message_add_on_type was 56 for all reaction rows; the value is also reported as stored. In tested one-to-one chats the add-on's sender_jid_row_id held -1 (55 of 55 rows), so the Sender columns resolve only in group chats; in a one-to-one chat the Chat JID column identifies the other party and the Direction column shows Outgoing where the add-on's from_me is 1 and Incoming where it is 0. Table and join layout informed by WAInsight, https://github.com/akhil-dara/WAInsight (MIT); the commit read is not recorded here.",
                        "category": "WhatsApp"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_whatsapp_message_receipts",
                        "tablename": "get_whatsapp_message_receipts",
                        "name": "WhatsApp - Message Receipts Per Recipient",
                        "description": "Rows of msgstore.db's receipt_user table: one per recipient per message, with its receipt_timestamp, read_timestamp and played_timestamp",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Codex, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-14",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "One row per recipient per message from the receipt_user table. The three timestamp columns carry the table's own column names (receipt_timestamp, read_timestamp, played_timestamp); the database does not document their exact semantics and this artifact does not assert them. Receipt rows whose message_row_id matches no row in the message table are kept, with the message fields empty (112 of 206 rows on one tested image). Table and join layout informed by WAInsight, https://github.com/akhil-dara/WAInsight (MIT); the commit read is not recorded here.",
                        "category": "WhatsApp"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_whatsapp_system_events",
                        "tablename": "get_whatsapp_system_events",
                        "name": "WhatsApp - System Events",
                        "description": "Rows of msgstore.db's message_system table with their message rows and companion tables",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Codex, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-14",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Rows in message_system joined to the message row and to the companion tables that hold decoded details where present: affected participants (message_system_chat_participant), old and new number (message_system_number_change), and prior value (message_system_value_change.old_data). action_type is an integer with no lookup table in the database and is reported as stored. Is Me Joined (as stored) is message_system_group.is_me_joined. The companion column names are this module's labels for the joined tables. A query that fails on an unexpected schema reports no rows and logs its SQLite error; an empty result is not evidence the table held nothing. Table and join layout informed by WAInsight, https://github.com/akhil-dara/WAInsight (MIT); the commit read is not recorded here.",
                        "category": "WhatsApp"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "appIdleStats",
                "module_filename": "appIdleStats.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "app_idle_stats",
                        "tablename": "app_idle_stats",
                        "name": "App Standby Buckets",
                        "description": "The App Standby bucket the platform recorded for a package under an Android user, the reason for that bucket, and the usage times it keeps alongside them, which are not wall-clock dates.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-09-06",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-09-06",
                        "notes": "Read from the per-user app_idle_stats.xml under the system users folder, one row per package element, with User ID taken from the folder the file sits in. The pattern is not anchored on a data/ prefix, because a raw userdata partition image carries the same folder without one, as samsungs20_a13 does. The file was plain XML on all 58 tested files and an ABX binary XML read is used when the file carries the ABX magic, a branch no tested image exercised. Of the 39 registered Android corpora 36 produced rows; the other 3 are partial extractions that do not carry the file.\nStandby Bucket is the bucket the platform had the app in, from the UsageStatsManager STANDBY_BUCKET constants, and all seven of EXEMPTED, ACTIVE, WORKING_SET, FREQUENT, RARE, RESTRICTED and NEVER appeared across the tested images. ACTIVE through RARE run from more recent to less recent use in the AOSP descriptions, RESTRICTED adds that the app has also been misbehaving in some manner, and NEVER is the value AOSP gives for an app that has never been used. EXEMPTED is not a recency value: AOSP describes it as the app being exempted for some reason with the bucket unable to change, and it was the second most common bucket across the tested images. Bucket Reason is decoded from the stored value, which the platform writes as hexadecimal without a prefix, as a main reason in the high byte and a sub reason in the low byte. The sub reason is scoped to its main reason, so the same low byte means different things under different main reasons, and for the two forced main reasons the low byte is a set of bit flags rather than a single value. Bucket Reason (as stored) carries the raw value, and a value whose main reason is outside the AOSP set is reported as stored: that happened on 3 of 19,629 rows, all carrying main reason 0x700 with the RESTRICTED bucket on two Samsung images, and it is not named here because no source for it was found. Reference: AOSP, UsageStatsManager, the STANDBY_BUCKET, REASON_MAIN and REASON_SUB constants and reasonToString, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/4e43ad12e1b211f15152e9c5b16b0fe88e6b93f3/core/java/android/app/usage/UsageStatsManager.java\nThe columns marked device-on ms and screen-on ms are not wall-clock times and must not be read as dates. AOSP records them on a timebase that accumulates device-on time across boots, computed as elapsed realtime minus a snapshot plus a stored duration, so a value is an offset within that accumulator and not milliseconds since the epoch. The largest seen across the tested images was 34,487,884,500, about 399 days of device-on time, which read as milliseconds since the epoch would decode to 1971-02-04 rather than to any time the device existed. Converting them would need the mScreenOnDuration and mElapsedDuration pair the platform keeps in the separate system/screen_on_time file, which this artifact does not read. They are reported as stored, and within one extraction they still order packages by how recently each was used. The platform never-used sentinel, Integer.MIN_VALUE, is left blank, which is why Last Used is populated on 5,202 of the 19,629 rows; small negative values are real and are kept, as on the Android 10 image. Reference: AOSP, AppIdleHistory, getElapsedTime and the attribute constants with their timebase comments, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/4e43ad12e1b211f15152e9c5b16b0fe88e6b93f3/apex/jobscheduler/service/java/com/android/server/usage/AppIdleHistory.java\nNext Estimated App Launch is the one wall-clock value in the file, milliseconds since the Unix epoch, rendered in UTC. AOSP describes it as the next estimated launch time for the app, so it is a value the platform wrote forward and not a record that the app was launched. Across the tested images it was present on 2,230 of 19,629 rows and ranged from 2023-03-28 to 2027-09-06, so it carried dates both before and after the extraction dates and the column is blank on most rows.\nBucket Expiry folds two schema generations into one column: AOSP version 1 of this file writes expiryTimes item elements, and the version before it wrote activeTimeoutTime and workingSetTimeoutTime attributes, both of which are read here. 52 of the 58 files read were version 1 and 6 were the earlier form. Last Used By User was absent on the Android 10 image. Last Restriction Attempt and Restriction Reason are set on 75 rows; a blank Restriction Reason is the platform having recorded no reason, which AOSP also renders as empty, and it does not distinguish that from the attribute being absent on the older schema.\nThis file names packages, not installed packages. Measured on the tested emulator: of the 317 and 278 package names it held for the two Android users, one in each was not installed for that user, and an app removed with pm uninstall was not named here while system/appops_accesses.xml on that same device still named it. So retention after a removal differs between the two files and was measured rather than assumed. Whether an entry is removed at uninstall or on a later write was not established.\nThe path pattern is tolerant, so a second copy of a user file in an extraction would add its rows again. None of the 39 corpora carried more than one per user, and since User ID does not separate two copies of one user's file, the Source File column names the file each row came from.",
                        "category": "Usage Stats"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "zoom",
                "module_filename": "zoom.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "zoom_account",
                        "tablename": "zoom_account",
                        "name": "Zoom - Account and Encrypted Stores",
                        "description": "Reports matching xmpp.zoom.us substrings from selected Zoom paths and the existing store-marker counts per app data directory.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-07",
                        "notes": "One row per app data directory that holds an account name or at least one counted store. The First xmpp.zoom.us Path Match column preserves the existing first matching substring from the selected paths. That substring alone does not establish an account identity. The xmpp.zoom.us Path Matches (JSON) column retains the existing pattern's non-overlapping finditer matches from every retained path, each paired with that normalized evidence-relative path, in retained path encounter order and then left-to-right match order. It is read from names because the stores themselves are encrypted; where no such name is present the column is empty and the counts still report what the directory holds. Encrypted Databases counts files whose name marks them as encrypted and which do not begin with the SQLite magic, and Encrypted Preference Files counts the preference files the app names with its encrypted prefix. Neither was recoverable from the tested extractions. The preference files are named with an enc_ prefix and were not readable on the tested extractions. Whether they follow AndroidX EncryptedSharedPreferences, which the AndroidX reference describes as an implementation of SharedPreferences that encrypts keys and values (https://developer.android.com/reference/androidx/security/crypto/EncryptedSharedPreferences), was not established here, and the module reads none of the key material. The counts are reported so an examiner can see how much is present and unreadable rather than being left to infer it from an empty report. Field mapping was done against three private samples provided by Mattia; no sample data is recorded for them. The appended field is compact JSON text containing an ordered list of objects with match and path string members; repetitions within and across retained paths remain. An emitted counted-store-only row with no matching name holds [] in this field. Existing canonical alias selection can remove paths before matching; this field does not recover excluded aliases or original path bytes. All four prior native values, row grain, marker/magic counts, first-five sorted display and first-fifty encounter-order source cap are unchanged. Account-only rows can still have empty Source Files and artifact source; candidate paths in the JSON field provide their own scoped path association, without changing that source policy. enc_ XML names and nonempty non-SQLite prefixes are the existing count tests and do not independently prove encryption, unreadability or completeness. Private-sample, AndroidX, library and name-derived identity assertions above remain historical and unverified by this correction. Original contribution credited to @AlexisBrignoni, @mattiaepi (Mattia Epifani) and Claude. No key material is read or decrypted.",
                        "category": "Zoom"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "keepNotes",
                "module_filename": "keepNotes.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_keepnotes",
                        "tablename": "get_keepnotes",
                        "name": "Google Keep Notes",
                        "description": "Parses Google Keep Notes",
                        "author": "Heather Charpentier",
                        "created_date": "2024-12-02",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-08-10",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Google Keep Notes"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "discordChats",
                "module_filename": "discordChats.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_discordchats",
                        "tablename": "get_discordchats",
                        "name": "discordChats",
                        "description": "Parses Discord chat messages from the kv-storage key-value store, including messages another state in its write-ahead log holds and the current state does not",
                        "author": "@abrignoni",
                        "created_date": "2023-09-18",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-26",
                        "notes": "Each account's kv-storage/@account.<id>/a database is read separately (one account on each of the 6 tested images holding one); Direction is Outgoing when the sender id equals the account id in that folder name. Attachment renders the first attachment whose URL path (scheme, host and path, ignoring the signature and size parameters) equals the URL of an image entry in the same container's OkHttp response cache (cache/http-cache, <hash>.0 metadata beside the <hash>.1 body), taking the largest cached copy; of the 12 attachments on the tested images 7 rendered, 4 had no cached copy and 1 was cached as a short text/plain response. Attachment Filename, Attachment URL and Attachment Proxy URL list every attachment as stored, joined with semicolons. The cache is the same store the Discord Cached Messages artifact reads, and on the tested images it held every kv-storage message but 2. On the tested images Edited Timestamp was blank, Pinned was False and Mention Roles was an empty list on every message, Mentions was an empty list on all but one image, on three images every message carried the same Avatar value, and on four the messages sat in one Channel ID. Conversation, Server Name, Server ID, Channel Name and DM Recipients come from the channel and server records the app caches in the kv_cache0 table of the same database, matched on Channel ID. For a server channel, Conversation is the server name (or the server id when no server record is cached) and #channel name; for a channel with no guild_id it is the channel name, or 'DM:' and the recipients from the record's rawRecipients (username and user id). When rawRecipients is absent the ids in recipients are used. The DM label is given to any cached channel with no guild_id and no name; the channel type is not checked. In tested samples every such channel had type 1, which the Discord Developer Documentation lists as a direct message between users (https://docs.discord.com/developers/resources/channel). When the channel is not cached those columns are blank and Conversation shows the Channel ID (not exercised on the tested images; every channel was cached). In tested samples every row resolved: 25 of 47 on pixel7a_a14 and 2 of 27 on falken_a326u_a13 in a server channel and the rest in a channel with no guild_id, and all rows on userb2_a13 (25), hc_pixel8pro_a16 (7) and samsungs20_a13 (1) in a channel with no guild_id. Channel Name was blank on every row in a channel with no guild_id. Record Origin. Live rows come from a normal read of messages0. Recovered rows do not: their key, columns a to f of messages0, is held by another state in the database's write-ahead log and absent from the current one. messages0 declares no primary key; the unique index messages0_by_key on a to f is what makes a row one message, so a row whose key the current state still holds is not reported, whatever its older versions held. Recovery Method and Recovery Location are filled in only on Recovered rows. Why a Recovered row is absent is not established here. States are read by scripts/sqlite_wal.py: each earlier committed state of the log's current generation (state n is the database after the first n committed transactions, and state 0 the database file without its log), the previous generation's left-over frames where its checks pass, and frames after the last commit. A Recovered row is reported once, as the newest committed state holding its key stored it, else the previous generation's newest left-over commit, else the frames after the last commit. Recovery Location names the committed states the key was read in, with their first and last numbers, and the previous log generation with the first and last numbers of its left-over commits; for a key read only after the last commit it names the log's frames after the last commit. Recovery Method is WAL committed states, or WAL uncommitted frames for a key read only after the last commit. The previous-generation and uncommitted-frame paths were not exercised on these images or by the unit test. On the tested images no Recovered row was returned, so Record Origin held Live on every row and Recovery Method and Recovery Location were empty on every row. falken_a326u_a13, pixel7a_a14 and userb2_a13 had a log beside the database, and every key held by the states that could be read was still current; on falken_a326u_a13 none of the previous generation's 18 commits could be read. samsungs20_a13 had no log; hc_pixel8pro_a16 and hc_pixel8pro_a17 had an empty (0-byte) log, which the reader treats as no log. The Recovered path is exercised by a unit test, not by these images.",
                        "category": "Discord Chats"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "ipMemoryStore",
                "module_filename": "ipMemoryStore.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "ipmemorystore",
                        "tablename": "ipmemorystore",
                        "name": "Wi-Fi IP Memory Store",
                        "description": "Rows of the NetworkAttributes table of the Android network stack's IpMemoryStore.db: per Wi-Fi network key, the IPv4 address, lease expiry and DNS servers of its last stored DHCP lease, with the network's SSID where it can be recomputed.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-10-29",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-29",
                        "notes": "Reads the NetworkAttributes table of the Android network stack's IP memory store in user_de/0 of com.google.android.networkstack or com.android.networkstack, IpMemoryStore.db or, after the network stack renames it, IpMemoryStoreV2.db (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/83ea07ed94890b52265e07d6760c7ed8f4cbd344/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#195, #297). Format and meanings come from AOSP NetworkStack at commit 44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452 and Wi-Fi at 82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60 (both android14-release); the network stack ships as an updatable module, so the version on a device can differ from its OS release; other versions were not compared, and vendor builds were not decompiled. One row per table row (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#66). Lease Expires is assignedV4AddressExpiry, milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#78): when DhcpClient saves a lease it writes the address, the DNS servers and the current time plus the lease duration in one record (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/android/net/dhcp/DhcpClient.java#846). It writes 1 for a lease it saw expire and Long.MAX_VALUE for an infinite lease (#275, #851); the column is blank for both, and neither value occurred on the tested images. Assigned IPv4 Address is assignedV4Address, stored as an integer by inet4AddressToIntHTH (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#263) and shown dotted, most significant byte first; DNS Servers is dnsAddresses, each address a length byte and its bytes (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#232). Record Expires is expiryDate, milliseconds since 1970-01-01 UTC (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#71): each time a record is stored for the key the store moves it forward by a relevance bump, at most 400 days from then (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreService.java#230, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/RelevanceUtils.java#302, #192). It is when the store would drop the record, not when the record was written (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/44e87d4600f9f3f746de6940a13863110a60c452/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#665), and the time of a write cannot be worked out from it alone. Group Hint and L2 Key are as stored. Wi-Fi's score card writes the group hint for a key (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#2226): G and the hex of the first 8 bytes of a SHA-256 over the device's ANDROID_ID, the network's SSID and the address 02:00:00:00:00:00, each part preceded by its length byte (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#301, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#2296, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#2339), the ANDROID_ID being read from the secure settings (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiInjector.java#371). L2 Key is W and the hex of the same hash (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#2066). For an access point record the BSSID replaces that address (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#782); for a per-network record the placeholder address is kept, so its L2 Key and Group Hint hashes are equal (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/Wifi/+/82d2b854425b2e8731f4902bbba175c653b54d60/service/java/com/android/server/wifi/WifiScoreCard.java#1084). On the tested images 170 of 1,270 rows were of the second kind, none with a lease. SSID is filled when the hash of a saved network matches the group hint: the module takes android_id from system/users/0/settings_secure.xml and every SSID in the WifiConfigStore*.xml files of the extraction, rollback copies included. The saved-network files carry a BSSID field that was empty in 180 of 182 elements on the tested images, so the module does not resolve L2 Key. A blank SSID means no saved network matched, not that the network had no name. Rollback snapshots of the store (misc_de/0/rollback/<id>/...) are read too: a snapshot row equal to a row of the live store in every other column is left out, and any other snapshot row is kept with Database naming the snapshot. The storage views one extraction can hold for an app data directory are read once. Not read: the PrivateData table (the Wi-Fi score card's per-key protobuf records), and in IpMemoryStoreV2.db the NetworkEvents table (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/packages/modules/NetworkStack/+/83ea07ed94890b52265e07d6760c7ed8f4cbd344/src/com/android/networkstack/ipmemorystore/IpMemoryStoreDatabase.java#156), which held no rows in any of the 8 copies on the tested images. On the tested images: 1,270 rows on 21 of 22 images (none on galaxys10_a10, where the search found no store); 1,236 named an SSID and 395 held an address, a lease expiry and DNS servers, all three or none. Of the 34 rows with no SSID, 24 had no group hint and 10 had a hint no saved network matched: 1 row on cookbook_a11, 5 rows (one hint) on pixel3_a11 and 4 rows (two hints) on samsungs20_a13. On samsungs20_a13 settings_secure.xml could not be parsed (a value holds unescaped quotes), so no ANDROID_ID was read; on cookbook_a11 and pixel3_a11 an ANDROID_ID was read and no saved SSID produced the hint. 80 rows on 4 images came from rollback snapshots, so Database held one value on the images without one. Lease Expires leads the table as the time tied to a connection, and is blank on the rows that hold no lease. On the emulator images emu_a15_oss2_v3 and emu_a15_oss_v17 the named network was AndroidWifi, the only network in their WifiConfigStore.xml.",
                        "category": "WiFi Profiles"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "PrivatePhotoVault",
                "module_filename": "PrivatePhotoVault.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "private_photo_vault_account",
                        "tablename": "private_photo_vault_account",
                        "name": "Private Photo Vault - Account Usage",
                        "description": "Install date and the key event count and last key event date the Private Photo Vault app keeps in its main preferences file, with the two dates converted as Unix milliseconds and the counts as stored.",
                        "author": "@Gear-I & Claude, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "notes": "No email or password account fields were found in this preferences file; these values are counters kept in the app's preferences, not a user identity. 'Key Event Count' and 'Last Key Event' come from a preference the app calls lastKeyEventDate/keyEventCount; its exact meaning is not documented publicly, so this module reports the raw value rather than asserting what triggers it. On the device this was validated against, its one recorded event's timestamp lands within a second of every imported media file's own creation timestamp, which does not establish what the event is. 'Uses Encrypted Preferences' is the app's own usesEncryptedPreferencesV2 flag, reported as stored; what the app does when it is set is not established here, and no PIN value was recovered from this file.",
                        "category": "Private Photo Vault"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "private_photo_vault_albums",
                        "tablename": "private_photo_vault_albums",
                        "name": "Private Photo Vault - Albums",
                        "description": "Albums recorded in Private Photo Vault's local database, each with its own bucket identifier and creation time.",
                        "author": "@Gear-I & Claude, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Read from the app's own ppv.db, opened read-only alongside its write-ahead log (ppv.db-wal) so that any transaction committed to the log but not yet checkpointed into the main file is still included. On the device this was validated against, two albums exist: one with bucket_id 'albums' holding the device's three imported pictures, and one, created one millisecond later, with bucket_id 'albums_decoy' and no media in it. This module reports the bucket_id exactly as stored rather than asserting what feature it belongs to. Creation Date (As Stored) is the creation_date text. Created is that text converted to UTC, and is filled only when the text carries a Z or a UTC offset; a value with neither is left blank in Created because no zone is recorded for it. Both stored values on pixel7a_a14 carry a UTC offset.",
                        "category": "Private Photo Vault"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "private_photo_vault_media",
                        "tablename": "private_photo_vault_media",
                        "name": "Private Photo Vault - Media",
                        "description": "Imported media files recorded in Private Photo Vault's local database, with each row's stored creation date, image width and height, and the favourite, deleted and view count columns as stored.",
                        "author": "@Gear-I & Claude, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2026-08-17",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Read the same way as Private Photo Vault - Albums, including the write-ahead log. The image and video content behind each row was not recovered: on the device this was validated against the files at File Path and Thumbnail Path do not begin with any recognizable image file signature, and while the database does store a per-file 'Encryption Key' and IV for each row, that stored value decodes from base64 to 48 bytes, which is not the length of a bare 32-byte AES key, and no key that decrypts the files was recovered from the extraction. The encryption_key and local_iv values are reported exactly as stored. How the 48 byte value relates to the key that encrypts the file is not established, and this module makes no attempt to decrypt the media itself. 'View Count', 'Favourite' and 'Deleted' are reported as stored; what updates them is not established. Creation Date (As Stored) is the creation_date text. Created is that text converted to UTC, and is filled only when the text carries a Z or a UTC offset; a value with neither is left blank in Created because no zone is recorded for it. All three stored values on pixel7a_a14 carry a UTC offset, two of them with a two digit fraction of a second.",
                        "category": "Private Photo Vault"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "chrome",
                "module_filename": "chrome.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_chrome",
                        "tablename": "get_chrome",
                        "name": "Web History",
                        "description": "Parses Web History from Chromium based browsers",
                        "author": "@abrignoni",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Chromium"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_chromewebvisits",
                        "tablename": "get_chromewebvisits",
                        "name": "Web Visits",
                        "description": "Parses Web Visits from Chromium based browsers",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-09",
                        "notes": "Transition (As Stored) retains the full visits.transition SQLite value without conversion. Core Transition Interpretation names the low byte of that value, 0 through 10, with the names Chromium's page_transition_types.h gives them at the commit cited below, without the PAGE_TRANSITION_ prefix (0 is PAGE_TRANSITION_LINK there and 6 is PAGE_TRANSITION_AUTO_TOPLEVEL). A low byte above 10 is left blank in that column and its number can be read from Transition (As Stored). The names are those of the cited commit; whether the browser version that wrote a row used the same names was not checked. Qualifier(s) decodes nine qualifier bits above the low byte, 0x00800000 BLOCKED through 0x80000000 SERVER_REDIRECT. The 0xC0000000 IS_REDIRECT_MASK is a mask covering the CLIENT_REDIRECT and SERVER_REDIRECT bits rather than a qualifier of its own, so it is not reported as a separate qualifier. Reference: Chromium, 'ui/base/page_transition_types.h', https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/8f4baaae073181e7e0fea1807f8db6ad720dbcb7/ui/base/page_transition_types.h#L30-L161",
                        "category": "Chromium"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_chromesearchterms",
                        "tablename": "get_chromesearchterms",
                        "name": "Search Terms",
                        "description": "Extracts text after the first ASCII case-insensitive search?q= marker in selected Chromium History URLs and decodes it once with unquote_plus. Search URLs of other forms are not reported; the keyword_search_terms table is reported by Keyword Search Terms.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-06",
                        "notes": "The existing SQLite LIKE substring selection is retained. The selected text ends at a second literal search?q= marker or the first ampersand, and is decoded once with unquote_plus: literal plus becomes space and %2B becomes plus. The raw URL is retained. A fragment is retained if it occurs in the selected text before an ampersand; the marker may occur outside a query parameter. A row does not establish a typed search or provider ownership. Other URL forms remain omitted. Original parser credit: @abrignoni.",
                        "category": "Chromium"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_chromedownloads",
                        "tablename": "get_chromedownloads",
                        "name": "Downloads",
                        "description": "Parses Downloads from Chromium based browsers",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-31",
                        "notes": "Tab URL is the downloads.tab_url column, the page the download was started from. The source URL of the downloaded file is held in the downloads_url_chains table, which this artifact does not parse. State, Danger Type and Interrupt Reason retain the stored downloads.state, danger_type and interrupt_reason values without enum interpretation. They do not establish completion, safety or user intent. Historical mapping research references (not verified across browser versions): Chromium, 'components/history/core/browser/download_constants.h', https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/8f4baaae073181e7e0fea1807f8db6ad720dbcb7/components/history/core/browser/download_constants.h; 'download_interrupt_reason_values.h', https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/8f4baaae073181e7e0fea1807f8db6ad720dbcb7/components/download/public/common/download_interrupt_reason_values.h; 'download_row.h', https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/8f4baaae073181e7e0fea1807f8db6ad720dbcb7/components/history/core/browser/download_row.h#L58-L60. ETag (as stored) and Last-Modified (as stored) are downloads.etag and downloads.last_modified, which DownloadRow describes as the contents of the most recently seen ETag and Last-Modified headers (https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/030bb0851e6c8c0fdd53d2270d5ae905a9dbce07/components/history/core/browser/download_row.h#L82-L86). The history schema migration from version 27 to 28 adds both columns as NOT NULL with an empty default (https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/030bb0851e6c8c0fdd53d2270d5ae905a9dbce07/components/history/core/browser/history_database.cc#L847-L850; https://github.com/chromium/chromium/blob/030bb0851e6c8c0fdd53d2270d5ae905a9dbce07/components/history/core/browser/download_database.cc#L206-L208), so on a History database from before that migration both are blank because the columns do not exist (no registered image carries a database that old, so that path is not exercised), and a blank on a later database means the column holds an empty string. The values are reported as stored; nothing is parsed from them. On the tested images an ETag was filled on 3 of 3 rows on adams_ss135dl_a13, 12 of 17 on cookbook_a11, 1 of 1 on hickman_a13, 2 of 4 on pixel7a_a14, 0 of 1 on russell_a14, 1 of 4 on s20fe_a13, 3 of 90 on sharon_a13, 4 of 108 on sharon_a14 and 1 of 1 on userb2_a13, and Last-Modified on 3, 15, 1, 4, 0, 2, 89, 107 and 1 of those rows.",
                        "category": "Chromium"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_chromekeywordsearchterms",
                        "tablename": "get_chromekeywordsearchterms",
                        "name": "Keyword Search Terms",
                        "description": "Parses Keyword Search Terms from Chromium based browsers",
                        "author": "@abrignoni",
                        "created_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2020-03-19",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Chromium"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "airtagAndroid",
                "module_filename": "airtagAndroid.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "airtagalerts",
                        "tablename": "airtagalerts",
                        "name": "Android Airtag Alerts",
                        "description": "Parses unknown-tracker (AirTag) alerts (creation and update timestamps, MAC address, device type and alert status) from the Google Play services personalsafety database.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2023-08-18",
                        "last_updated_date": "2025-03-16",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Airtag Detection"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "airtagscans",
                        "tablename": "airtagscans",
                        "name": "Android Airtag Scans",
                        "description": "Parses unknown-tracker (AirTag) scan records (timestamps, MAC address, state, and three values decoded without a schema from the bleScan and locationScan blobs) from the Google Play services personalsafety database.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2023-08-18",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Possible RSSI is field 2 of the bleScan blob. Possible Latitude and Possible Longitude are fields 4 and 5 of the locationScan blob divided by 10,000,000. Both blobs are decoded without a schema, and the meaning of those fields and the scale are not established by a published schema. On the 86 rows of kevin_pocox7_a15, pixel7a_a14 and sharon_a14, field 2 was a whole number from -98 to -27, and fields 4 and 5 divided by 10,000,000 fell within -90 to 90 and -180 to 180 on every row. The values were not compared with another location record of those devices, so no map output is produced from them.",
                        "category": "Airtag Detection"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "airtaglastscan",
                        "tablename": "airtaglastscan",
                        "name": "Android Airtag Last Scan",
                        "description": "Reports field 1 of the personalsafety_info protobuf file, read as Unix milliseconds.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2023-08-18",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "The file is decoded without a schema. That field 1 is the time of the last scan is not established by a published schema. On kevin_pocox7_a15, pixel7a_a14 and sharon_a14, field 1 read as Unix milliseconds was 200 seconds after, 23 seconds before and 72 seconds before the newest creationTimestampMillis in the Scan table of personalsafety_db, so it did not equal the time of the newest stored scan record on any of the three.",
                        "category": "Airtag Detection"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "gmsScreenlockStateHistory",
                "module_filename": "gmsScreenlockStateHistory.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "gmsscreenlockstatehistory",
                        "tablename": "gmsscreenlockstatehistory",
                        "name": "Google Play Services - Screen Lock State History",
                        "description": "Entries of the screen lock state history that Google Play Services keeps in its DeviceStateSyncManager preferences: the time of the last check in each run of one state, whether the device was secure at that check, and how many checks the run had.",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-10-26",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-26",
                        "notes": "Reads screenlock_state_history and acknowledged_secure_lock_state from Google Play Services' com.google.android.gms.auth.authzen.cryptauth.DeviceStateSyncManager preferences (plain XML; the ABX reader is present in the code but no tested file was ABX); one row per history entry, in stored order, Entry 1 being the oldest kept. The meanings below come from the Play Services build installed on pixel7a_a14 (24.26.32, base.apk SHA-256 06c467b1bc80356cf530c5775d7ce76a757d5e58765c813c3098f75eb766005e), whose classes2.dex was decompiled with jadx 1.5.6; other builds were not decompiled. The two keys are also present in the 21.09.15 build on galaxys10_a10 and the 26.26.34 build on hc_pixel8pro_a17, which shows the keys exist there, not that the logic is the same. In that build DeviceStateSyncManager$CheckStateEventIntentOperation is registered, in the APK's ChimeraManifest.pb (decoded without a schema), for android.intent.action.USER_PRESENT, which Android sends when the user is present after the device wakes up, for example when the keyguard is gone (frameworks/base tag android-14.0.0_r1, commit 299fe6f5d6fc6f1af7c3411dcf4e5efdf7217368, https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/299fe6f5d6fc6f1af7c3411dcf4e5efdf7217368/core/java/android/content/Intent.java#2539). On that broadcast it sets an inexact alarm of type 3, ELAPSED_REALTIME, which is not delivered while the device sleeps (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/299fe6f5d6fc6f1af7c3411dcf4e5efdf7217368/apex/jobscheduler/framework/java/android/app/AlarmManager.java#139), for 15 seconds later, so a check runs at least 15 seconds after the broadcast and possibly much later. A check is skipped when the newest entry is less than 30 seconds old. Otherwise it reads KeyguardManager.isDeviceSecure(), true if a PIN, pattern or password was set, a locked SIM not counting (https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/299fe6f5d6fc6f1af7c3411dcf4e5efdf7217368/core/java/android/app/KeyguardManager.java#697). If the result equals the newest entry's, that entry is replaced with the current time and its count plus one; otherwise a new entry with count 1 is added; only the newest 5 are kept. Last Check in Run (timestamp_millis, read as Unix milliseconds) is therefore the time of the last check in a run of one state, not when the state began, and a change between consecutive entries happened after the earlier entry's time and no later than the later entry's time. Checks in Run is the number of checks in that run, not a count of unlocks: skipped checks, and broadcasts whose alarm never fired, are not counted. The history is reset to empty when the newest entry is later than the current clock or the stored text does not parse, so a short history is not evidence of a short period. Device Secure (as checked) and Checks in Run are is_secure and count as stored. Acknowledged Secure Lock State (as stored) is acknowledged_secure_lock_state, one value per preferences file repeated on each of its rows: the build writes it after a successful sync of the state to the server (the code's own wording; the endpoint was not traced) and removes it when the state changed during that sync. It was present in every tested file and held true on 28 and false on 7 of the 35 rows. On sharon_a14 and hc_pixel8pro_a17 the last check of a not-secure run came 78 and 51 seconds before the time the Lock Settings artifact reports as LSKF Last Changed for user 0, and the next entry was secure. Across the 35 rows of the tested images Device Secure (as checked) held true on 24 and false on 11, Checks in Run ranged from 1 to 3,017, and no user held more than 4 entries. Android User is the user whose app data folder holds the file; the same file under data/data and data/user/0 is read once, and the second users on cookbook_a11 and samsungs20_a13 (150) and russell_a14 and russell_pixel6a_a13 (10) were reported separately, so Android User held 0 on every row of the other images. An entry that is not a JSON object is reported as a row with blank time, secure and count values and logged; none was seen in the tested images.",
                        "category": "Device Settings"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
