                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_emulatedsmeta_audio",
                        "tablename": "get_emulatedsmeta_audio",
                        "name": "Emulated Storage Metadata - Audio",
                        "description": "Parses media store metadata (audio)",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni",
                        "created_date": "2020-10-19",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-27",
                        "notes": "Reads each com.google.android.providers.media.module external.db found, including a second Android user's, and takes one copy where one user's database appears under more than one of the data/data, data/user/N and data_mirror paths. A copy under data/misc_ce/N/rollback/, where installd's snapshotAppData copies an app's credential encrypted data, is read too unless it is byte identical to the live database of the same user. A row from that copy is left out when the live database of the same user gave a row equal to it in every column but Source DB Path, each live row answering for one copy row, so what remains from the copy is what the live database does not hold in the same form, under the copy's path in Source DB Path. On the 7 tested images that hold rollback snapshots, 6 of them a copy of this database, this removed 1,826 of 4,555 rows across the Emulated Storage Metadata artifacts (the legacy Files artifact had no rows on these images) and kept 90 rows from copies, 88 of them in Files (pixel3_a12 54, russell_a14 15, hickman_a13 12, hc_pixel8pro_a16 7). Reference: AOSP, 'InstalldNativeService::snapshotAppData', https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/native/+/2827a4a16b0340ecd07c2d5a6c89991799b362bb/cmds/installd/InstalldNativeService.cpp#1676 The Source DB Path column names the database each row came from, and the report's 'located at' line lists every database read, including any that returned no rows. MediaStore records DATE_ADDED and DATE_MODIFIED in seconds since epoch and DATE_TAKEN in milliseconds, and this parser decodes them that way. Key Timestamp is not a stored column: it shows Date Added, or Date Modified when Date Added is zero or absent. Date Taken is shown in UTC; the Android reference says an image must carry both the EXIF original time and its offset for this value to be reliably placed in relation to the epoch, so where the file had no offset the instant is not established. Reference: AOSP, 'MediaStore.MediaColumns', https://developer.android.com/reference/android/provider/MediaStore.MediaColumns",
                        "category": "Emulated Storage Metadata"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "roles",
                "module_filename": "roles.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_roles",
                        "tablename": "get_roles",
                        "name": "roles",
                        "description": "Parses the roles in the roles.xml file, one row per returned direct-child name occurrence, with the source path variant, user and role. A role with no returned child name has one blank row.",
                        "author": "@abrignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2021-01-25",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-09",
                        "notes": "Source Path Variant records the collected path, not an Android version. Every direct child with a name attribute is reported in XML order, including blank names, duplicates and named children with other tags. A blank Holder row means no child name was returned, not proof that no holder exists. Missing child or role name attributes are diagnosed; malformed children or roles alone are skipped. Source File is included only when rows come from multiple distinct evidence-relative origins. Historical Anne, HC Pixel and Samsung A53 runs had no multiple-holder role; that boundary uses constructed XML. Files under a path component named mirror are skipped. A copy under data_mirror/misc_de/<volume>/<user> is not read when the extraction also holds data/misc_de/<user> roles.xml for the same user beside it; the skip is written to the run log and the two copies are not compared. On samsunga53_a14 the archive listing gives both copies the same size and CRC-32, and the artifact returned 76 rows before this rule and 38 with it. Any other copy under data_mirror is read.",
                        "category": "App Roles"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "packageGplinks",
                "module_filename": "packageGplinks.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_packagegplinks",
                        "tablename": "get_packagegplinks",
                        "name": "packageGplinks",
                        "description": "Parses installed package names and a Google Play Store address this module builds from each name. The names come from the system packages.list; the address is not stored there and is not checked to exist.",
                        "author": "@abrignoni",
                        "created_date": "2021-03-18",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-09",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Installed Apps"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "googleLastTrip",
                "module_filename": "googleLastTrip.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_googlelasttrip",
                        "tablename": "get_googlelasttrip",
                        "name": "Google Maps Last Trip",
                        "description": "Values decoded from Google Maps' saved_directions.data.cs: two places with coordinates (rows marked Start and End) and two timestamps, protobuf field 5.1 on the Start row and field 4 on the End row, read as Unix milliseconds. No source for the field meanings is cited; what the times mark is not established.",
                        "author": "@abrignoni",
                        "created_date": "2023-10-16",
                        "last_updated_date": "2023-10-16",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "GEO Location"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "googleChat",
                "module_filename": "googleChat.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_googlechat",
                        "tablename": "get_googlechat",
                        "name": "Google Chat - Messages",
                        "description": "Rows of topic_messages in dynamite.db joined to Groups and users; a message whose group or creator is not in those tables is not reported. Messages and earlier message text another state in the write-ahead log holds and the current state does not are reported too. Direction is derived, not stored: Outgoing when the creator id equals the user id of the account e-mail named in the database path, Incoming otherwise, and blank when that account cannot be resolved.",
                        "author": "Josh Hickman & Alexis Brignoni",
                        "created_date": "2021-02-05",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-26",
                        "notes": "Record Origin. Live rows come from a normal read of the query. A message is the unique index of topic_messages on (group_id, topic_id, id), not row_id: on hickman_a13 12 of 39 messages held a different row_id in different states. A Recovered row is a message another state in the database's write-ahead log holds and the current topic_messages table does not. An Earlier version row is a non-empty text_body another state held for a message whose current text_body differs, one row per distinct text. A row whose message and text the current state still holds is not reported again. The whole query is run in each state, so a Recovered or Earlier version row's sender and conversation come from the same state as its text. Recovery Method and Recovery Location are filled in only on those rows. Why a message is absent or its text changed is not established here. States are read by scripts/sqlite_wal.py: each readable earlier committed state of the log's current generation (state n is the database after the first n committed transactions, and state 0 the database file without its log; states the reader cannot rebuild exactly are skipped and counted in the run log), the previous generation's left-over frames where its checks pass, and frames after the last commit where SQLite's quick_check passes. Each row is reported as the newest committed state holding it stored it, else the previous generation's newest left-over commit, else the frames after the last commit. Recovery Location names the committed states the row was read in, with their first and last numbers, and the previous log generation with the first and last numbers of its left-over commits; for a row read only after the last commit it names the log's frames after the last commit. Recovery Method is WAL committed states, or WAL uncommitted frames for a row read only after the last commit. On pixel7a_a14 the reader read 4 of the previous generation's 17 left-over commits and no row came from them. No row on the tested images or in the unit test went through the previous-generation or uncommitted-frame wording of Recovery Method and Recovery Location. On the tested images no Recovered or Earlier version row was returned, so Record Origin held Live on every row and Recovery Method and Recovery Location were empty on every row. The Recovered and Earlier version paths are exercised by a unit test and by the same code in iLEAPP's Google Chat artifact, not by these images.",
                        "category": "Google Chat"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_googlechat_groups",
                        "tablename": "get_googlechat_groups",
                        "name": "Google Chat - Groups",
                        "description": "Rows of the Groups table in dynamite.db with the creator's name from the users table. A group whose creator is not in users is not reported.",
                        "author": "Josh Hickman & Alexis Brignoni",
                        "created_date": "2021-02-05",
                        "last_updated_date": "2021-02-05",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Google Chat"
                    },
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "get_googlechat_users",
                        "tablename": "get_googlechat_users",
                        "name": "Google Chat - Users",
                        "description": "Google Chat users (dynamite.db)",
                        "author": "Josh Hickman & Alexis Brignoni",
                        "created_date": "2021-02-05",
                        "last_updated_date": "2021-02-05",
                        "notes": "",
                        "category": "Google Chat"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "mozillaProfileTimes",
                "module_filename": "mozillaProfileTimes.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "mozillaprofiletimes",
                        "tablename": "mozillaprofiletimes",
                        "name": "Mozilla Browser Profile Times",
                        "description": "The created and firstUse values of each Mozilla-based browser profile's times.json (Firefox, Tor Browser and other apps that keep a files/mozilla/<profile> folder).",
                        "author": "@AlexisBrignoni, Claude",
                        "created_date": "2026-10-30",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-30",
                        "notes": "Reads times.json in each files/mozilla/<profile> folder of an app's data directory, one row per profile. Mozilla's ProfileAge module writes this file (https://github.com/mozilla-firefox/firefox/blob/204a96d56b40285148fea160628bbad54ff98f3c/toolkit/modules/ProfileAge.sys.mjs): when created is missing and the profile's creation time is asked for (lines 85-98), it stores the oldest timestamp among the files in the profile folder at that moment, using the file creation time where the platform has one and the modification time otherwise (lines 13-45 and 136-145), and when firstUse is null it stores the current time (lines 59-62). Android builds were not decompiled, so whether they reach that code is not established. Created is the created value read as milliseconds since 1970 UTC and shown to the second. On all 12 tested profiles it was within one second of the oldest modification time, in UTC, among the profile folder's files in the image (the file times were 0.1 to 1.0 seconds earlier), and that oldest file was times.json itself. It is not established as the time the app was installed or first opened. First Use (as stored) is the firstUse value: it was null on every tested profile that had the key and absent on pixel3_a11. Other Keys (as stored) lists any further keys, such as reset or recoveredFromBackup, which ProfileAge writes; none were present on the tested images. App Package is the folder the profile sits in, so a Firefox fork with its own package name is reported under that name; on the tested images the packages were org.mozilla.firefox and org.torproject.torbrowser. Android User is the user whose storage holds the file, and the duplicate storage views of one file (data/data, data/user/0, data_mirror) are read once: the 20 tested files gave 12 rows, two of them for Android user 10.",
                        "category": "Mozilla Browsers"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "googlePlaySearches",
                "module_filename": "googlePlaySearches.py",
                "artifacts": [
                    {
                        "artifact_key": "googleplaysearches",
                        "tablename": "googleplaysearches",
                        "name": "Google Play Searches",
                        "description": "Rows of the suggestions table in the Google Play Store suggestions.db: the date (read as Unix milliseconds, UTC), display1 and query columns",
                        "author": "Alexis Brignoni, @AlexisBrignoni, Codex",
                        "created_date": "2020-04-02",
                        "last_updated_date": "2026-10-04",
                        "notes": "Every suggestions.db the paths match is read, once per Android user: where an extraction holds the same user's file under more than one storage path (data/data, data/user/N, data_mirror/data_ce) one copy is read. Source File names the database each row came from. On russell_pixel6a_a13 the file exists for Android users 0 and 10 with 12 and 10 rows; samsungs20_a13 holds a second copy under user/150 with 1 row beside the 21 of the main copy. Timestamp is the date column read as Unix milliseconds and shown in UTC to the whole second; the column is declared LONG and held a 13 digit integer on every row of the 20 databases read from 17 registered zip extractions. A blank or non-numeric date is left blank. The table's CREATE statement declares display1 UNIQUE ON CONFLICT REPLACE, so one display1 text has at most one row in a database. What causes the app to write a row is not established.",
                        "category": "Google Play Store"
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "module_name": "googleDuo",
